<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Parakeet.Engine.LlamaServer</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One project owns one engine's interop, as Parakeet.Engine.ParakeetCpp owns parakeet.cpp's and
    Parakeet.Engine.Python owns the sidecar. What this one owns is llama.cpp's `llama-server` as a
    bundled child process — the v2 language-model tier, decided in docs/V2-ASK-THE-TRANSCRIPT.md
    decision 1: the app starts llama-server.exe from native/win-x64/llm/<backend>/ on a loopback
    port with an api-key, talks HTTP to it, and kills it on exit. No struct layout crosses the
    process boundary, which was the point of choosing the child over a binding — and why this
    project has no native interop beyond the kill-on-close job it shares with the sidecar through
    Parakeet.Core.

    Parakeet.Core declares IAnswerEngine and knows nothing about processes, ports or SSE; this
    project implements it and nothing else of Core's. No package references: the HTTP client and
    the JSON reader are the BCL's.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Parakeet.Core/Parakeet.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Parakeet.Engine.LlamaServer.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
